# Build outputs
/pulse
/pulse.exe
/bin/

# Test artifacts
*.test
*.out
coverage.txt

# Local state (never committed; secrets never touch disk by design)
.pulse/

# Agent-environment vendoring (ponytail plugin + third-party skills).
# Restore with the installers in ~/.local/share/opencode-skill-src/ (MEMORY.md ADL-0013).
.opencode/
opencode.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Markdown lockdown
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AGENTS.md §4.6 defines a COMPLETE documentation surface: seven Essential Vibe
# Coding Files and nothing else. Ignoring *.md by default makes that structural
# rather than aspirational — a stray notes file, a scratch README inside a test
# fixture directory, or a vendored third-party README cannot be committed by
# accident, and cannot be mistaken for state a reviewer is expected to read.
#
# The default-deny is the point. A repository that lists what it tracks is a
# repository where every unlisted file is a decision nobody made; a repository
# that lists what it does NOT track is one where every tracked file was.
#
# Two git semantics decide whether this rule actually does what it looks like
# it does. Both were verified by `git check-ignore` rather than assumed, and
# the first one was WRONG on the first attempt:
#
#   1. A `!pattern` containing no slash matches at EVERY depth. An unanchored
#      `!README.md` therefore resurrects `vendor/README.md` and
#      testdata/fixture/README.md — which is the precise thing this rule
#      exists to prevent. Every negation below is anchored with a leading
#      slash so it matches only at the repository root.
#   2. A `!pattern` cannot rescue a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded,
#      because git never descends into an excluded directory. No directory is
#      excluded here, so root negations apply normally.
*.md

# --- the tracked documentation surface ---
# Six negations. Anything else ending in .md is denied by the rule above.
# README.md and CONTRIBUTING.md do not exist yet; their negations are
# permissions, not files.
!/AGENTS.md
!/PRD.md
!/ARCHITECTURE.md
!/README.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/LICENSE

# --- tracked but NOT whitelisted, and why that is not a contradiction ---
# Four of the seven Essential Vibe Coding Files - DESIGN_SYSTEM.md, ROADMAP.md,
# TASKS.md, MEMORY.md - plus GLOSSARY.md and the three .agents/*.md config files
# are tracked in HEAD while this file no longer whitelists them.
#
# THIS IS NOT A LIE, IT IS GIT SEMANTICS: an ignore rule only ever governs
# UNTRACKED files. A tracked file stays tracked no matter what .gitignore says,
# so dropping these negations denies every FUTURE .md file without untracking a
# single existing one. The working set is unchanged.
#
# Actually untracking them is a SEPARATE, DESTRUCTIVE step (`git rm --cached`)
# and was NOT taken here, for two reasons that are load-bearing rather than
# stylistic:
#
#   * scripts/ci/doc-sync-gate.sh greps
#     `^(AGENTS|PRD|ROADMAP|ARCHITECTURE|DESIGN_SYSTEM|TASKS|MEMORY)\.md$`
#     over `git diff --name-only`, which only ever lists TRACKED files. Untrack
#     those four and the gate can never match, so every commit touching cmd/ or
#     internal/ would fail CI permanently - the gate would reject correct work
#     for the reason that the work carried no documentation, which is the
#     opposite of what AGENTS.md 4.6 asks of it.
#   * MEMORY.md is the append-only ADL ledger. ADL-0015 - the rule that makes
#     origin/main canonical and forbids force-pushing it - lives there, so
#     untracking it removes the provenance rule the repository is built on.
#
# To genuinely slim the public surface, the change belongs in doc-sync-gate.sh
# and AGENTS.md 4.6, not in this file.

# --- local agent scratch ---
# HANDOFF.md and anything like it: working notes for one session, never state.
# A handoff should POINT at TASKS.md or MEMORY.md, both of which are tracked.
/HANDOFF.md
